  "text": "It is pointed out that the variables used to evaluate the quantum action integrals do not have to obey the usual equations of motion, and a broad rule for picking such variables is presented. The rule states that any collection of momentum‑like and coordinate‑like quantities that (a) follow the canonical motion equations, (b) allow the problem to be separated into independent parts, and (c) have an average kinetic energy equal to one‑half the sum of each momentum times the time derivative of its partner coordinate, will produce the correct energy spectrum when inserted into the condition that the integral of momentum over coordinate equals an integer multiple of the fundamental constant. This family of coordinate systems includes the familiar normalized angle‑type variables and the ordinary generalized coordinates that permit separation. By merging the principle of stationary action with a known quantization technique for systems that are only conditionally periodic, one obtains a single variational statement that simultaneously yields the allowed classical trajectories and the quantized orbit sizes and shapes. The approach is demonstrated on a nonlinear oscillator whose restoring force contains both quadratic and quartic terms."
